-- title:
--   Finite point sets lie in affine opens over an affine base open
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $C$ a scheme, $c : C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a morphism, and $\varepsilon$ a section of $c$, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ whose composite with $c$ is the identity. Let $\mathfrak F$ be a finite-map datum for $(c,\varepsilon)$: opens $U, V \subseteq C$, sections $f \in \Gamma(C,U)$ and $g \in \Gamma(C,V)$ and a natural number $m$, such that $U$ and $V$ are affine open with $U \sqcup V = \top$, a point of $C$ lies in $U$ exactly when it is not in the image of the base map of $\varepsilon$, $U \cap V$ equals both $C_f$ and $C_g$, the restrictions of $f$ and $g$ to $U \cap V$ multiply to $1$, the $R$-algebra maps $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,U)$ and $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,V)$ sending $X$ to $f$, resp. $g$ (the algebra structures being those induced by $c$) are finite, and for every local $R$-algebra $S$ and every $s \in S$ the quotient of $S \otimes_R \Gamma(C,U)$ by the ideal generated by $1 \otimes f - s \otimes 1$ is a finite free $S$-module of rank $m$. Then for every affine open $V_0$ of $\operatorname{Spec} R$ and every finite set $F$ of points of $C$ with $c(x) \in V_0$ for all $x \in F$, there is an open $U_0 \subseteq C$ which is affine open, satisfies $U_0 \le c^{-1}(V_0)$, and contains every point of $F$.
--
--   This is the standard quasi-projectivity statement that a finite set of points of a curve admitting a finite map to $\mathbb P^1_R$ lies in a single affine open, here in the relative form demanded over an affine open of the base. It supplies exactly the covering hypothesis used in the construction of the scheme of relative effective Cartier divisors, and is cited in the representability and properness results for the relative Picard functor of a curve carrying a finite-map datum.
